+
+# Implement JSON Pointer resolvePointer (RFC 6901)
+
+## Request
+
+Our services need a standard way to refer to a value inside a JSON document. Implement JSON Pointer,
+as specified in RFC 6901.
+
+Export a function `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from `src/index.ts`. Given a JSON document and a
+JSON Pointer string, it returns the value the pointer refers to. It throws an error when the pointer is
+not a valid JSON Pointer, or when it does not resolve to a value of the document.
+
+This is the first of a few features that build on each other; the next ones will use it.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+- `resolvePointer("", ...)`: the empty pointer returns the whole document.
+- Reference tokens are separated by `/`; `~1` decodes to `/` and `~0` decodes to `~`, applied in that
+  order (RFC 6901 section 4).
+- Array indices: a token is either `0` or a non-zero digit followed by digits (no leading zeros, no
+  sign). `-` refers to a non-existent element and does not resolve, so it throws.
+- A non-empty pointer must start with `/`; any other string is invalid and throws.
+- A `~` not followed by `0` or `1` is invalid and throws.
+- Missing object keys, out-of-range or non-numeric array indices, and descending into a primitive
+  throw.
+- Object keys are looked up as own properties only (no prototype lookups such as `constructor`).
+
+## Scope
+
+- Library code in `src/index.ts`, no runtime dependency.
+- Tests with `node --test` covering the RFC 6901 examples and the error cases above.
